[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 2"""
 date="2026-10-02T14:33:10Z"
 content="""
I have thought of several different ways to support concurrent tests with TAP
output:

1. Include a TAP parser (or at least a parser for the subset of TAP that
   tasty-tap uses, which does not include YAML blocks), so subtest
   process output is gathered, parsed, and re-emitted. (Also needs a TAP
   generator.)
2. Add an option like `--test-portion=1/3` so the caller can run multiple
   processes in parallel and do whatever combining they like. This is close
   to the way subtest processes are implemented currently, so probably the
   easiest option. Also more flexible since it can be used without using
   TAP.
3. Since test numbers are actually optional in TAP output. modify tasty-tap
   to not include test numbers, and then the outputs of concurrent
   processes can be concacenated easily. This would probably entail forking
   tasty-tap, but it is a small amount of code, so total code would
   probably be less than option #1.
4. Clean up git-annex's hacks around parallelism and tasty by making
   tasty itself support running the sub-test processes. That would leave
   tasty-tap reporting on the collection of all the tasty test results at
   the top level. This would be the best solution.

However, I think this should be deferred to a later todo if it turns out to
be needed.
"""]]
